# Worker role — {{CAMPAIGN_TITLE}}

The per-experiment procedure for the agent that executes one unit and exits: the iteration contract, the
loop steps, how to judge the result you got, and what must be on disk before you finish. The planner
never reads this file.

The shared contract is not repeated here. The campaign definition (metric, levers, caps, promotion
rules) is `campaign.md`; the hard gates are `CLAUDE.md`. Both reach you every cycle: `CLAUDE.md`
auto-loads, and the driver compiles `campaign.md` into your prompt.

## The iteration contract — what this unit must persist for the next one
Under `--supervise`, each iteration runs in a fresh agent context, and the ledger is the loop's only
memory. Work you did but did not record does not exist for the next iteration. The driver enforces its
hard core structurally: a unit that leaves it incomplete is retried, then shelved with its failure
recorded as `kind: "operational"` in `queue.json`, while the loop continues. Only several such failures
in a row, or an integrity condition, halts the loop. Before an iteration ends, it must have persisted:

| What | Where | Checked by |
|---|---|---|
| The save-set: result JSON + figure + snapshot | `results/` (the runner's record gate) | driver + runner |
| Every failed mechanical check explained, not dismissed. The runner writes them; you answer them | `results/checks_<NN>.json` → the logbook Analysis slot | runner (writes) + `campaign-status` (audits) |
| A completed logbook entry — the two slots you owe, Analysis (one paragraph; every failed check answered by name) and NEXT (one line), placed through `--record`; the runner writes the rest, and a blank Analysis is not saved | `results/logbook.md` (via `--record`) | driver (structural: the entry and a non-blank Analysis) + `campaign-status` (content) |
| The JOURNAL — the campaign's working memory — brought up to date, with any landmine, anomaly, or cross-experiment observation written down; the next iteration reads only what is on disk, and a unit that leaves the file untouched is not saved | `results/JOURNAL.md` | driver (touched) + `campaign-status` (content) |
| Your unit's outcome recorded: if you DECLINE it, or PROPOSE follow-up units, `--record <exp> --decline "<why>"` / `--propose <file>` (the runner drops the JSON in `results/queue_drop/`; the driver merges it and removes/updates the queue — you never edit `queue.json`) | `results/queue_drop/` (via `--record`) | driver |
| A declination or ESCALATE, if either happened, with reasons | `results/queue_drop/` (decline) / `results/ESCALATE.md` | driver |

The narrative-quality half of the contract is `campaign-status`'s audit, not the driver's: whether each
slot is filled well, and whether a landmine is actionable. The driver checks structure; humans and
checkpoints check substance.

## The Experiment Loop
```
Experiment 000 (ONCE, before the loop): BASELINE GATE
   {{BASELINE_COMMAND}}
   -> establishes {{PRIMARY_METRIC}} ≈ {{BASELINE_VALUE}} (the floor) and validates the
      {{VALIDATION_CHAIN}} chain end-to-end. The runner writes its ledger row; `--record` it and
      bring the JOURNAL up to date like any unit.
     (`campaign.md` §Baseline gate)

WHILE not interrupted:
  1. HYPOTHESIZE & PREDICT — Pick the change (campaign.md §The levers). Before
                    running, write predictions/<exp_name>.json in the shape this campaign's
                    prediction_mode requires, with a ONE-SENTENCE rationale: the number and the one
                    basis it rests on (a unit, a bound, a prior exp id, a literature key). The
                    prediction written first is what makes the comparison a test; the sentence is the
                    control, not an essay.
  2. IMPLEMENT    — {{IMPLEMENT_VERB}}, in scripts/experiment.py (the hooks and your helpers; never
                    the runner); new config configs/exp<NN>_<slug>.json (copy _template.json).
                    List the levers this config moves in its `_levers`; the runner cross-checks the
                    list against the keys that actually changed (`CLAUDE.md` HARD GATE 2). One-line
                    `_comment` and `_hypothesis`; nothing else is free text. `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` grades
                    what moving several cost you.
  3. {{RUN_STEP_NAME}}  — {{RUN_COMMAND}}   (the FROZEN metric; obey the caps in campaign.md).
  4. EVALUATE     — {{PRIMARY_METRIC}} (PRIMARY) + {{SECONDARY_METRICS}} via {{EVAL_WRITER}}.
  5. {{SANITY_STEP}}    — {{GUARDRAIL_CHECK}} (logged, not primary).
  6. LOG          — the runner writes the ledger row (results/scoreboard.tsv; schema in campaign.md)
                    and ends the run with the result CARD: the primary metric and its delta vs the
                    incumbent, the guardrail, expected vs found, every failed or unavailable check with
                    its `needs`, what changed. Read it once (`--card <exp>` prints it again); never
                    rebuild the comparison from the artifacts with one-liners, and never open the
                    scoreboard or the logbook.
  7. ANALYZE      — Start from the card: the runner has already judged whether the
                    change beat this campaign's own resolution ({{SPREAD_NOUN}} spread, or the
                    numerical tolerance if this campaign is deterministic) and whether the result met
                    the prediction in whatever shape this campaign's prediction_mode requires
                    (results/checks_<NN>.json holds the detail when a card line is not enough). Every
                    failed check is yours to explain before moving on; a flag you did not answer is
                    not a passed check. Then the judgement no code makes: did the guardrail hold, and
                    is the effect statistical or systematic? Then RECORD your own checks, directed at
                    what this unit changed (the artifact's `changes` block names every file and config
                    key that moved): for each change, what you expected it to do and what you observed;
                    the checks you ran (a recompute, a limiting case, a sum rule). Write them FIRST,
                    before any narrative, with
                    `python scripts/run_campaign.py --self-check <exp> --ran name=pass|fail|skip[:detail]
                    ... --change "what=>expected effect=>observed" ... --note "<one line>"` - an
                    artifact, never prose. It raises the result's label to `self-checked` (and the
                    logbook's Verification line with it); only an independent verdict raises it further.
                    The Analysis you write next may cite it instead of restating it.
  8. ARTIFACTS    — every experiment: save {{ARTIFACT_LIST}}, a cheap visualization, and the
                    reproducibility bundle (config + code snapshot) under results/snapshots/exp<NN>_<slug>/.
                    Noteworthy results get additional visualizations. (§The save-set, below)
  9. RECORD       — the runner already wrote this experiment's entry in results/logbook.md (facts,
                    Expected vs found, Levers, a pending Verification line) and its ledger row (`what`,
                    `expected_vs_found`). Everything you owe is ONE file and ONE command: write
                    results/record_<NN>.json with the Write tool — `self_check` {ran, changes, estimate,
                    note}, `analysis` (one paragraph: every failed check answered by name, then your
                    judgement), `next` (one line), `ledger_note` (≤200 chars the numbers do not show,
                    optional), `finding` (one line citing results/verdicts/<id>.json, only when a
                    verdict settled something), `propose` / `decline` — then `python
                    scripts/run_campaign.py --record <exp> --from-file results/record_<NN>.json`. The
                    runner records the self-check, places the slots and the cells, drops the proposal;
                    validated and idempotent, a re-run replaces. (The same things exist as flags;
                    `--help` lists them.) Update ../../../PROJECT.md if a finding changes the physics.
 10. JOURNAL      — write the new results/JOURNAL.md whole, with the Write tool, in one turn. The
                    previous one is in your brief and the driver archived it and removed the working
                    file before you started, so the Write needs no read. Carry forward what your unit
                    did not change; update the NEXT line, Where we stand, Live hypotheses, What to try
                    next and why (consistent with what you proposed), Traps and tooling, Open questions,
                    Anything else. Keep it under ~120 lines (the brief clips it). Never re-read it to
                    check the write. A unit that leaves no new JOURNAL is not saved.
 11. CHECKPOINT   — if exp_id % {{CHECKPOINT_EVERY}} == 0, run `/physkit:campaign-status`: stop
                     feature-chasing and reorient. Is the campaign still healthy, and still pointed at
                     the goal?
 12. REPEAT      — the next experiment begins at step 1. Supervisor mode (the default): the driver
                    repeats, and you, a fresh-context worker, execute steps 1–11 for your one unit and
                    exit once the iteration contract validates. That end-of-turn is earned. Only in an
                    attended by-hand session does REPEAT fall to you, and there a completed experiment
                    is still not a stopping cue (CLAUDE.md HARD GATE 5).
```

## How the loop runs — supervisor mode
The loop above is the per-unit procedure; who repeats it is the mode:

- Supervisor mode (`{{PYTHON_CMD}} scripts/run_campaign.py --supervise --backend <codex|claude>`) is the default for unattended
  runs. A deterministic driver pops one unit at a time from `results/queue.json` and spawns a fresh
  agent context per unit: you, the worker, execute steps 1–11 for one unit and exit. Nothing carries
  over between units. The active instruction twin auto-loads, and the driver compiles your brief into
  the prompt: this file, `campaign.md` (minus its append-only record), the project's standing
  constraints, the JOURNAL (the campaign's working memory, whole), your unit's queue entry and config,
  and the ledger's last rows. Orient from the brief, never from the tree; §Where to look names the few
  reads outside it.

  **Keep the solve alive under your own session — never fire-and-forget it.** A single tool call is
  capped (~10 min on every platform), so a long fit cannot be one blocking call — but the fatal error
  is *exiting while it is still solving*: the driver reads your exit as the unit's completion, and a
  solve you backgrounded (`nohup` / `&` / `Start-Process`) then walked away from loses
  `results/.run_heartbeat` (the driver's dead-worker signal) and never lands its save-set, so the unit
  fails `saved=False` — and you are **not** re-invoked when it finishes. On Windows a POSIX-backgrounded
  solve (`nohup`/`&`) does not even survive your exit; it is reaped with your session. So run the solve
  so it keeps the heartbeat live and **stay alive until the save-set is on disk** — block on it, or
  background it and poll (a `Monitor` until-loop) within your `iteration_timeout_hours`; never exit while
  it is still solving, and do not noop-poll with `echo .`.

  **Kill a subprocess by the PID you captured at launch — never by grepping the process list.** Under the
  default `auto` permission mode, a `kill`/`taskkill` on a PID found via `tasklist`/`ps` is denied as
  killing an unowned process (it is how a worker once killed the driver); killing the exact PID you
  started is allowed. Same for a detached solve — keep its PID in view so the cleanup reads as yours.

  Two rules follow:
  - Mid-unit anomalies: probe through the pattern, not around it. Document the problem + hypothesis
    in the JOURNAL and propose a standard probe unit (`--record <exp> --propose <file>`); a claimed
    major problem needs a verify verdict
    before the plan may steer on it. Trivial sanity checks are free. Anything that could influence a
    conclusion or consume real time is a unit.
  - A planner agent (fresh context, read-mostly, strategy-only) refills/reorders the queue on
    triggers: queue low, every k units, a declination, a plateau, a REFUTED verdict, after a promotion.
    Direction judgment lives there and in your proposals, never in the driver.

Do not pause to request more data, a different metric, or a radically different objective. Work
strictly within the levers in `campaign.md` §The levers.

The stall release is a failure signature, not an exit. After roughly 25 continuations with no new
scoreboard row, the Stop hook releases the session and stops blocking it. That is not a third way for
the loop to end; the two ways are in `CLAUDE.md` HARD GATE 5. It is the hook reporting that a loop is
alive and producing nothing, which is a dead run. Diagnose the stall when the warning first appears
(`/physkit:debug`). Do not grind to the release. If this session is not the loop at all (you are
answering a question, fixing setup, or reading the ledger), record `not-a-loop-session` rather than
letting the counter run.

## Metric integrity (the FROZEN metric) — don't move the goalposts, don't unfairly punish a valid model
The metric is gameable. The characteristic failure is a change to what the metric sees rather than a
change in system performance. Two opposite failures share one root cause: a mismatch between what is
measured and true performance.

(1) The metric flatters a result unfairly (goalpost-moving). Before trusting any score, ask:
- Did this experiment change anything the metric/guardrail consumes ({{METRIC_INPUTS}}) in a way that
  flatters the number without improving true performance? A score is comparable to baseline only
  if the metric saw the same kind of input.
- Is it apples-to-apples with exp 000: same {{COMPARABILITY_AXES}}, same scoring path? If not, part of
  the delta is the change in measurement, not the method.

(2) A guardrail punishes a valid run unfairly. A structurally incompatible check, one hard-wired to an
assumption an allowed lever legitimately changed, measures the mismatch rather than a real failure.
When you suspect this:
- Flag it in the JOURNAL and the ledger's `note` cell (`--record <exp> --ledger-note`). Never accept the
  penalty silently, and never work around it.
- Build a fair evaluation that reflects the run's actual settings, and re-score. The fix must be more
  faithful to what was tested, never merely more lenient. If you cannot tell which, treat it as gaming
  and keep it flagged.
- Mark resulting findings PROVISIONAL until the fairness is independently reviewed/verified; only then
  may they be written as a ledger `finding` cell.
- Hard boundary: you may adapt the mutable guardrails and diagnostics for fairness; you never edit the
  FROZEN primary metric, the data, or known ground truth on your own. A metric you believe is WRONG is a
  different case with its own procedure: §Metric epochs, next.

## Metric epochs — when the FROZEN metric itself is wrong
Frozen means locked against quiet change, not immutable. A campaign sometimes discovers its ruler is
wrong: the scorer measures the wrong thing (noise-free data when the campaign targets low SNR; a proxy
that overfits and needs a second gate), or it does not compute what `campaign.md` §The ONE metric
specifies. Revising it openly is the highest-integrity move available; revising it quietly is the
failure the freeze exists to prevent. The procedure:

1. **Is it really the metric?** A mutable guardrail or diagnostic that misjudges a run is fixed in place
   (§Metric integrity (2), no epoch). An experiment that changed what the metric sees is §Metric
   integrity (1). Only a fault in the FROZEN scorer or its specification opens an epoch.
2. **Verify the claim before acting on it.** "The metric is flawed" is a kill-claim against the current
   metric and gets `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}`'s kill-claim check like any other invalidation; the epoch cites
   the verdict artifact. A `semantics` revision (what the metric measures changes, so §The ONE metric is
   edited) needs a REFUTED or EVIDENCE_AGAINST kill of the old metric. A `mechanics` fix (the
   specification stands; the scorer did not compute it) needs the correctness check that reproduces the
   discrepancy, kept in `tests/correctness_checks.py` as a regression.
3. **Name the direction.** `harder` (the current best would score worse under the new ruler), `neutral`,
   or `easier` (it would score better). Easier is the goalpost-moving shape: it needs a REFUTED-tier
   kill and gets maximum scrutiny at ratification.
4. **Who enacts it is `configs/loop.json` `campaign.metric_revision` — the user's dial, never yours to
   change:**
   - `cautious` (the default): write `results/ESCALATE.md` with a `## METRIC-REVISION PROPOSAL` block —
     the claim; the verdict artifact; the proposed change (the specification diff and the scorer diff);
     `kind` and `expected_direction`; the re-baseline cost (which retained contenders must be re-scored).
     Finish your unit if you can. The driver halts at the next loop top; the user opens the epoch or
     declines it.
   - `autonomous`: enact it yourself, in this order — bump `campaign.metric_version`; add the entry to
     `campaign.metric_epochs` (`kind`, `status: "pending"`, `evidence`, `expected_direction`,
     `opened_by: "worker@<exp_name>"`); edit the specification (`campaign.md` §The ONE metric, for a
     semantics epoch) and the scorer; `python scripts/metric.py --selftest` green; then run the runner,
     which re-runs the correctness suite and binds the new epoch's scorer hash; then queue the
     re-baseline (step 5). The guard refuses an entry with no evidence, an easier revision without a
     REFUTED kill, and any entry at all when the dial is `cautious` — propose instead.
   - **The user already opened it** (a `cautious` proposal accepted: `campaign.metric_epochs` declares
     the current `metric_version` but the scorer on disk is still the previous epoch's): enact it now,
     in the `autonomous` order above, *before running any unit* — the runner binds an epoch's scorer
     hash on the first experiment under it, so a unit run before the scorer edit would bind the new
     epoch to the old scorer.
5. **Re-baseline; never compare across the boundary.** The retained contenders are
   `configs/RECOMMENDED.json` plus every ledger row carrying a `finding` cell or still cited as live in
   `results/JOURNAL.md` §Where we stand or `results/queue.json`. Re-score them under the new ruler as
   ordinary units — one config-backed unit per contender, sharing a `block` — and propose them through
   `--record <exp> --propose <file>`. Until then the runner sets earlier rows aside and the checks
   artifact names the boundary. Rows keep their `metric_version`; nothing is deleted or re-labelled.
6. **One line in `results/JOURNAL.md` §Where we stand:** `metric epoch <version> opened (<kind>,
   <direction>) · <verdict or check> · <what the new metric measures, one sentence>`. That line is the
   narrative record; the evidence is the verdict, the change is the specification diff, the governance
   is the entry.

The epoch stays `pending` until the user ratifies it — from an attended session, never a spawned role —
and a ratified or rolled-back epoch is final. A pending epoch promotes normally, with its standing
riding on the row's version (promote-and-label); a rolled-back one never promotes. When the ruler was
wrong but the save-sets are valid and re-scorable, this procedure applies; when the artifacts
themselves are tainted (a forward-model bug, corrupted data), that is `fork-campaign`, not an epoch.

## After the run — reconcile with the prediction, and check cheap before you spend
You wrote a predicted range before running (`campaign.md` §Bound expectations BEFORE you run). Hold the
result against it:
- Update the bounds as understanding improves, but treat any result that lands outside the predicted
  range as a red flag deserving extra scrutiny. Do not move on until you understand the source of the
  surprise. A result that beats the predicted range is the most common signature of a leak or an
  accounting error.
- Settle statically-answerable questions with a cheap check before spending an expensive run. A
  surprising or non-monotonic scan result is often an analysis or selection artifact: a noisy
  single-epoch checkpoint pick, an off-by-one, a stale cache, visible in an existing run's logs at zero
  extra compute. Inspect that first. Do not launch further multi-hour runs to explore a wiggle that a
  one-line log check would have explained.

## Decision-making guidelines
- Primary up, guardrail holds → log, queue a confirmation / seed-repeat.
- Only {{CHEAP_SUBGROUP}} improved → cheap or near-trivial; not a real win, keep exploring.
- Primary up, guardrail degraded → suspect {{OVERFIT_MODE}}; second seed / control.
- Flat or ambiguous → after 2–3 inconclusive scouts, move to an orthogonal lever.

## Before writing off or accepting a result — every result is suspect until cross-checked
Before writing off an idea (a flat or negative result): could it be implemented differently
(parameterization, init, regularizer, optimizer, or the order of steps)? Is there a bug or confound (a
sign/convention slip, a pose/parameter mismatch, a degenerate setting)? Did it interact badly with a
fixed choice that should co-vary (test the pair, not the part)? Was the effect within replicate noise?
A crash is a bug, not a verdict: fix it and re-run before judging the idea. Do not dismiss a weak
signal as noise: real effects can be small and noisy, so gather more evidence before concluding noise.
Was the test run in a regime where the competing hypotheses are distinguishable? A null measured where
the effect vanishes under either hypothesis proves nothing. Example: testing a stiffness difference
only in saturation, where both stiff and soft predict no canting. Rule the idea
out only from a regime where it would have shown, had it been real.

Before accepting a positive result, ask the same, plus: did the experiment move the goalposts
(re-check §Metric integrity)? Did it land inside the range you predicted? Is it a ceiling move
(physical) or a gap-to-ceiling move (suspect)? Does the guardrail pass? Does it generalize across the
whole {{GENERALIZATION_AXIS}}, not a lucky subset? Confirm with ≥2–3 independent experiments and the
independent verification the preset requires before calling a method the best or writing a DISCOVERY
(under `minimal`, the claim audit at the end of the campaign is that verification).
Counter confirmation bias explicitly, as a ritual: state the result you hoped for, make the strongest
case that it is wrong, name the one observation that would refute it (and check you ran it), and give
a fair hearing to the unwelcome alternative conclusion (it is noise; a rival explains it too; it is an
artifact). Depth: `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` §5c. Whatever you conclude, the self-check you record (loop step 7)
is what a reviewer reads first: a claimed effect with no change behind it, or a change with no stated
effect, is a red flag in its own right.

## Rigor — replicate before concluding

> First check which campaign this is. `configs/loop.json` `campaign.noise_model` says whether
> repeated runs of this campaign differ at all. In a deterministic campaign they do not: seeds and
> replicate spread are not the test, `campaign.tolerance` is, and multi-seed work here buys nothing
> but spend. Everything below is written for the stochastic case; take the parts that apply.
> Replicating a deterministic pipeline does not make it more certain, it makes it slower.

- Replicate with perturbed parameters and, for any stochastic step, multiple seeds, to tell a real,
  robust effect from run-to-run noise. A change smaller than the replicate spread is not a result.
- Could the effect be a seed or init artifact? Confirm with multiple seeds and inits before any hard
  conclusion, positive or negative.
- Track error and separate its kinds. Report each metric with a spread. Statistical error shrinks with
  more seeds or frames: run more. Systematic error is a convention, pose, or calibration bias: more
  runs will not help, so find and fix the cause. {{SYSTEMATIC_AXIS_NOTE}}
- Use a statistical test, not eyeballing, for close calls. Compare per-{{UNIT}} distributions
  (paired) rather than two point means; a difference must clear the spread.
- [PROPOSED] Define and report effect size. State the minimum meaningful Δ`{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}`
  (≥ 2× the replicate std) up front, and report deltas as `Δ ± spread`.
- This is required before (a) writing a finding into its ledger row's `finding` cell, or (b)
  permanently writing off an approach. One run never justifies either.

## Independent verification — assume the result is wrong and try to refute it
Convergence is not correctness. Before any finding is written into its ledger row's `finding` cell or
accepted as current-best, it must be independently re-derived. `campaign.md` §Verification lists this campaign's
own checks, decided at setup (for sim-heavy work the V&V&UQ method is the physkit plugin's
`docs/SIMULATION_VALIDATION.md`); `verify` reads them, `correctness-suite` encodes the
checkable ones as standing regression gates.

**In supervised mode, routine verification is the DRIVER's job, not yours — and how much of it runs is
the campaign's verification preset** (`configs/loop.json` `verify.preset`;
the physkit plugin's `docs/reference/verification-presets.md`). When the preset owes your accepted unit an independent check,
the driver spawns a verification agent (re-executing the unit when it is cheap and reproducible,
reviewing it from the frozen record otherwise), stamps the scoreboard's `verified` column from the
verdict artifact, and writes the unit's label into its checks artifact — you never grade your own work,
never lower the preset, and do not run verify at the end of a routine unit. A unit the preset owes
nothing keeps the label your self-check gave it (`self-checked`, or `unverified`), and that label travels
with the number; a promotion under `minimal` still says `unverified`, because a self-check is not
independent. You DO still invoke `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` yourself when something inside your unit demands
it before you act: a result that beats its envelope, a suspicion the metric leaked, a mid-unit claim you
are about to build on. In an attended session there is no driver, so the pre-promotion verification
below is yours to run, at the depth the driver would have decided (re-execution when the unit's measured
solve is within a quarter of `worker.iteration_timeout_hours` and `campaign.reproducible` is true; review
from the frozen record otherwise — the checks artifact's `verification.re_execution` says which).
- Validate every fit even when it converged cleanly: re-run from the config, recompute the metric from
  scratch, confirm it reproduces within tolerance.
- Falsify with code, not prose, and confirm the numbers are real. Write verification scripts and
  controls (edge cases, randomized inputs, counterexamples) rather than arguing in text. Confirm every
  reported number was produced by the evaluation: re-run it, for example re-running inference on the
  test set. Never trust a number that was only stated.
- Spawn an independent agent to refute it. Use the `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` skill: it dispatches a fresh
  agent instructed to assume the result is wrong, which re-runs the experiment, recomputes
  `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}` and the guardrail, replicates with perturbed params, checks for metric
  contamination{{VERIFY_EXTRA_CHECKS}}, and returns CONFIRMED / PROVISIONAL / QUESTIONABLE ASSUMPTION /
  REFUTED / INCONCLUSIVE
  with evidence. Where the preset requires it, a promotion is not real until this returns CONFIRMED;
  under `minimal` a promotion is real only stamped `unverified`. Run that verifier on a model at
  least as capable as the loop; a different capable model is preferable, for mechanism diversity. Never
  use a weaker model: a weak verifier yields false CONFIRMs. Mechanical sub-tasks the loop delegates
  (search, citation checks, figure generation, validation) can run on a lighter model, since a
  deterministic gate or your own read catches a slip.
- Scale verification to the run cost. When an experiment is fast, reproduce it fully and add multiple
  seeds + a statistical test. When it is long or expensive and a full re-run is not practical, the
  verifier instead scrutinizes the code and the conclusions/logic and re-checks the saved artifacts (models,
  data, logs) rather than re-running from scratch. That is why those artifacts must be saved
  (§Where everything is saved). The verify skill adapts its depth to the runtime.
- Verify the fairness of any adapted evaluation. If a result relied on an adapted guardrail (§Metric
  integrity, case 2), the verifier must judge whether the adaptation was faithful or merely lenient.
  Such findings stay PROVISIONAL until that judgment returns faithful.
- Record disputes, append-only. Every issue the verifier or the user raises lands on the experiment's
  logbook `Disputes & responses` line, each with a response (the verifier writes its concern and the
  response there; a user's challenge you answer is acknowledged in the JOURNAL under `## Open
  questions`). Never delete a raised issue; rebut it if you disagree. These records are part of the
  project's scientific integrity.

## When something looks unphysical — re-verify the FROZEN model first
Frozen is not validated. A forward model, data generator, or scorer must pass its correctness gate
before it is frozen and exp 000 runs: a sign/convention round-trip against a trusted analytic
reference, plus known-symmetry self-tests. Build and run those with `correctness-suite`. Freezing an
unvalidated model bakes its bugs into every downstream experiment.

Treat any later unphysical symptom (a sign flip, a reversed or mirrored artifact, a structured residual
that does not go away) as a trigger to re-verify the FROZEN model first, before building corrective
physics (tilt terms, detrending, extra capacity) on top of it. A persistent model–data gap can be a bug
in the frozen reference code, not only model-form error.

## Recording a promotion
Promotion is enforced, not only described. The verify skill writes a verdict artifact
`results/verdicts/<id>.json` (verdict, kill tier, named refuter). In supervised runs the driver
stamps the scoreboard `verified` column from that artifact after each block's verification; in an
attended session, record it yourself the same turn you promote — a row acting as current-best, or
carrying a `finding` cell, with a blank `verified` is a hygiene failure that `campaign-status` flags.
The PreToolUse hook (`scripts/guard_promotion.py`) guards the mechanical promotion surfaces,
**subject-matched**: a write to `configs/RECOMMENDED.json`, or a scoreboard edit that introduces a
`CONFIRMED` or `NEW BEST` token, needs — wherever the preset requires one — a CONFIRMED verdict with a
named refuter **for the experiment being promoted**, no other experiment's verdict counts; under a
preset that requires none (`minimal`) the write must carry the word `unverified` instead; recording a
system-level finding in the
project overview (`../../../PROJECT.md` §Updates from discoveries) must cite the verdict artifact by
path (a legacy project's `../../../DISCOVERIES.md` write stays hook-gated and fail-closed). The ledger's
`finding` cells are runner-written, not hook-gated, and the JOURNAL is working memory — but the same
rule binds both: every finding cites its verdict, an INCONCLUSIVE kill never lands, and
`campaign-status` audits uncited cells. Run the verify; do not work around the hook.

## The save-set — what every unit writes

### `results/JOURNAL.md` (the campaign's working memory)
One bounded document, delivered whole into every brief and brought up to date at the end of every unit:
the NEXT line, §Where we stand (established and ruled out, each with its confidence and the ledger id or
verdict), §Live hypotheses, §What to try next and why, §Traps and tooling, §Open questions, §Anything
else. Edit what changed, leave the rest, keep it under ~120 lines; the driver archives every version to
`results/journal_archive/`. The settled record is not here: a settled fact is its ledger row's `finding`
cell, written through `--record <exp> --finding "..."` only after multiple consistent experiments
confirm the finding on `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}` (guardrail noted) and `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` returned
CONFIRMED; the cell cites `results/verdicts/<id>.json` — hard gate #1 in `CLAUDE.md`. Under a preset that
owes no independent check (`minimal`), findings wait for the claim audit and cite its artifact
(`results/verdicts/audit_<NN>.json`) instead. Full narratives are not copied into the JOURNAL. When a
finding changes the physical understanding, also update `../../../PROJECT.md` (the effect, its
equations, references — §Updates from discoveries, citing the verdict; and the §Standing constraints
inventory, per its guarded tier).

### `results/logbook.md` (append-only — the full narrative record)
Every experiment's full entry, verbatim, forever. The runner writes the facts of each entry and the
judgements it can make from the artifacts (Expected vs found, Levers, a Verification line raised by the
self-check and the verdict stamp); you complete Analysis and NEXT through `--record`. Append-only, and
never opened: `--show <exp>` prints one entry when an old detail is needed.

### Where everything is saved (verify each is written every run)
Per experiment, all of these must exist (the label is the config stem `exp<NN>_<slug>`, which prefixes
every artifact): `configs/<label>.json` · a `scoreboard.tsv` row · `results/result_<NN>.json` ·
`results/checks_<NN>.json` ·
`results/images/<label>_*` (the basic figure) · `results/snapshots/<label>/` (config + code copy) · a
`results/logbook.md` entry (its Analysis and NEXT through `--record`) · the JOURNAL brought up to date.
Four of those are HARD GATE 4, enforced in code — the scoreboard row, `results/result_<NN>.json`, the
figure and the snapshot; the runner refuses the row until the last three exist. The rest are the
checkpoint contract, audited by `campaign-status` rather than blocked by the runner. Verify the whole
set after each run; if a run died before writing them, treat it as not-done and re-run.
- Tee every run's stdout to a file: `... > results/diagnostics/<stem>_<UTCstamp>.log 2>&1`. Never
  rely on console scrollback surviving.
- Any diagnostic that yields an actionable number must be persisted the same turn: append a line to
  `results/diagnostics/<name>.jsonl` and record it + the producing script in JOURNAL.md. A console
  print alone does not count as saved. Version diagnostic scripts; do not overwrite them in place.
- Log the conditions you were not varying (the "thermostat in the box"): exact config, command line,
  wall-clock, comparison baseline, and [PROPOSED] environment provenance (key library versions, host,
  RNG seeds).
- Reproducibility is the invariant — every experiment (baseline). For every experiment, the
  documentation of what was done and the exact code + data to redo it are preserved and self-contained
  in a per-experiment snapshot `results/snapshots/<label>/` (config + the mutable code that produced it +
  a manifest: command line, key library versions, RNG seed / data hash).
  Pin the code behind the number, not just the params; shared scripts get edited later. Git is optional
  (recommended for shared/large projects); the per-experiment snapshot is the reproducibility baseline
  either way (see the physkit plugin's `docs/reference/version-control.md`). Detail:
§The reproducibility bundle, below.
  {{CODE_SNAPSHOT_RULE}}
- Name everything by its experiment. Every artifact (config, snapshot, result JSON, figure,
  diagnostic log, saved model/data) is keyed by `<label>` (figures `results/images/<label>_*`), so every
  file traces to one experiment and to its row in `results/scoreboard.tsv`. No orphan files.
- Save quicker-check artifacts, not just the recipe, so a result, and a long run the verifier has
  to check, can be re-checked without rebuilding the pipeline. Key them by `<label>` and note where
  they went in the experiment's `results/logbook.md` entry. What counts: §Quicker-check artifacts.

### The reproducibility bundle — every experiment (baseline, non-negotiable)
Every experiment must stay reproducible even after the shared code/config are later edited. For each
experiment, save under a per-experiment directory `results/snapshots/exp<NN>_<slug>/`:
- a copy of the config that produced it, so the bundle is self-contained,
- a copy of the mutable code that produced it: the `scripts/` that ran (metric.py, experiment.py, run_campaign.py,
  and any campaign-specific module / forward model); a shared script edited later would otherwise make
  this experiment irreproducible,
- a short manifest: the exact command line, key library versions, and any RNG seed / data hash.
One directory per experiment, named by its label; keep it organized. The snapshot must be self-contained
and documented: a reader with only this directory can both understand and re-run the experiment. This is
the reproducibility baseline; git is optional and complements it, never replaces it (see
the physkit plugin's `docs/reference/version-control.md`). For external/large input data, pin it by
source + checksum +
generation command + seed rather than copying it in.

### Basic visualization — every experiment (baseline, fast)
Save at least one computationally cheap visualization every experiment so the user can follow
progress, even on a flat or negative result: {{FIGURE_SPEC}}
