## Where to look — the brief first; the tree only for these
Your spawn prompt already carries the campaign definition, this file, the standing constraints, the
whole JOURNAL, your unit, your code (`scripts/experiment.py`), and the ledger's last rows. Re-reading any
of them from disk buys nothing and costs the turn; reading the tree to orient (the logbook, the
scoreboard, another campaign, the runner's or the scorer's source) is the failure this brief exists to
end. A unit reads outside the brief only for:
- The result CARD the runner prints at the end of your run (`--card <exp>` prints it again): the
  comparison, the checks, what changed — read once, never rebuilt from the artifacts.
- `results/checks_<NN>.json` — what the runner mechanically checked for YOUR experiment, and what it
  could not check, when a card line is not enough: a `fail` is a bug or a discovery and is yours to
  explain, and an `unavailable` names in its `needs` field exactly what this campaign would have to
  supply to make that check real. Neither is a passed check.
- `python scripts/run_campaign.py --show <exp>` — one old logbook entry, for one named old detail.
  Never open `results/logbook.md` or `results/scoreboard.tsv`, to read or to write: the runner writes
  both, and the record you owe goes through `--record`.
- `../../../PROJECT.md` below §Standing constraints — the equations and methods, the instrument
  characterization, the predictive framework, the durable decisions, and §Lineage (what sibling
  campaigns tried and found). Open it only when a result needs physics the brief does not carry.
- `configs/loop.json` `campaign` — whether a prediction must carry a numeric range, and whether a win
  is judged against a replicate spread or a numerical tolerance (a small read; the rest of the file is
  the driver's).
- `python scripts/run_campaign.py --help` — the flag list. Your code is `scripts/experiment.py`, delivered
  in the brief and the one script you edit; the runner's source is the kit's machinery, not reading
  material, and neither is the scorer's: `campaign.md` §The ONE metric is the metric's specification.
